NAME
    networking - TOS zero-trust networking concepts

DESCRIPTION
    TOS networking is zero-trust: every remote machine starts as UNKNOWN and can
    only be pinged. Raising trust is always a deliberate LOCAL admin action.

TRUST LEVELS
    BLOCKED   Packets dropped silently.
    UNKNOWN   Ping/pong only (the default for everyone).
    KNOWN     + hostname exchange and public info.
    TRUSTED   + encrypted messaging, file transfer, remote execution.

    Manage trust with the 'net' command:
      net                 status
      net peers / scan    discover and list peers + their trust level
      net trust <addr>        raise to KNOWN
      net trust <addr> full   raise to TRUSTED
      net block <addr>        block a peer
      net revoke <addr>       drop back to UNKNOWN

THE WIRE
    Between TRUSTED peers (with encryption on), sensitive payloads are encrypted
    (AES with a data card; a MAC-protected XOR fallback otherwise) and carry a
    per-packet nonce plus an HMAC over (type, to, algo, epoch, seq, nonce,
    ciphertext). The receiver verifies the MAC before decrypting, rejects
    duplicate nonces and stale sequence numbers (per-peer counter + per-boot
    epoch), and refuses an XOR downgrade when AES is available. Discovery and
    handshake packets are intentionally public.

    Challenge-response binds trust to possession of the shared secret, not just a
    modem address — so a relocated trusted modem in someone else's hands cannot
    act as the trusted peer.

TOOLS
    ping <addr>                    Reachability check.
    chat                           TUI chat with trusted peers.
    rsh <addr> <cmd>               Remote shell on a TRUSTED, verified peer
                                   (sandboxed, step-budgeted). Off until the
                                   rshd service is started.
    scp <addr>:<path> <local>      Copy a file from a peer (/public by default).
    hostname [name]                Show or set this machine's hostname.

